 ######tIsLinux
≥3minor≥31≥3newauthor≥31≥2host≥2222.248.18.10≥2username≥2≥2revision≥28≥2id≥2111≥2tscreate≥21095268319≥2keepts≥21100503181≥1cache_diff_default_major≥11,13c1,229
< #REDIRECT OpenSourceFAQ/WhatIsLinux
< 
< = What is Linux and why should I use it? =
< 
< Linux is an operating system as Unix, MS DOS, and MS Windows are. This is the base software that runs and coordinates all the hardware to make the hardware basically useable. Then to use the system, you run application software to accomplish a particular type of task-- word processing, accounting, drawing, and so on. The underlying operating system software can be small and 'simple' as MS DOS or can be large, complex, and more useful as Linux. You should use Linux because it is 'free' and it works as at least as well and often better than other operating systems and offers your choice of several desktop configurations for ease of use.
< 
< Linux (or more specifically the main portion of the operating system, also known as the kernel) was created in 1991 by Linus Torvalds, a Finnish university student with a desire to create a new Unix-like operating system. Since then, the Linux project has been worked on by a large number of people, who have continued to improve the kernel as well as develop new programs for the operating system.
< 		
< There are many reasons for the everyday person to use Linux. The reason most often given is that the operating system and a set of programs (usually together in a package called a distribution) can be downloaded for no money off the Internet or purchased from a variety of retailers off the Internet for a small amount, usually less than 10 dollars US. While this is a good selling point for people who are new to the system, there are many other solid reasons to use Linux:
< 		
< #Runs on a variety of hardware- Linux can be adapted to run on almost any system built since its creation in 1991, thus letting people continue to use working hardware that otherwise might have to be replaced.
< #Access to the source code- Unlike many other operating system, the everyday user had access to the source code for the entire operating system, allowing the more technically-inclined to take a look, make modifications and perhaps even contribute to the development of the system.
< #Better security- Largely as a result of the number of people examining the code for Linux at any one time, security issues tend to be resolved quickly, with updates being freely available. Additionally, very few computer viruses in existence target Linux-based systems and most distributions make it possible for you to make security on your computer as tight as you wish in a fairly simple manner.
---
